C = [
("h1", "6. The Bridegroom in the Gospels"),
("p", "Claim: in the Gospels the figure of the bridegroom is taken from the prophets and applied to Jesus, and it is spoken to Israel. John the Baptist calls himself the friend of the bridegroom, and Jesus calls those who are with him the children of the bridechamber. In the parables of the wedding the hearers are guests, servants and virgins who go out to meet the bridegroom. The bride herself is never identified with any of them. The Gospels take over the figure of the husband and leave the wife where the prophets put her."),
("sub", "6.1 The friend of the bridegroom"),
("p", "The Baptist’s disciples come to him troubled that the one to whom he had borne witness is now baptizing and that all men come to him (John 3:26). John answers with the figure of the wedding: “He that hath the bride is the bridegroom: but the friend of the bridegroom, which standeth and heareth him, rejoiceth greatly because of the bridegroom’s voice: this my joy therefore is fulfilled” (John 3:29). He has just reminded them what his own place is: “I am not the Christ, but that I am sent before him” (John 3:28). Two roles are set side by side. One is the bridegroom, who has the bride. The other is the friend, who stands and hears and rejoices. John takes the second and gives the first to Jesus."),
("p", "Three points matter for the volume. The figure is the prophets’ figure, and John uses it without explanation because his hearers know it. The bridegroom is Jesus, so the husband of Israel in Isaiah and Hosea is the one whose way John was sent to prepare. And the friend is not the bride. The forerunner stands beside the wedding as a friend and rejoices in the voice, and he does not call himself the one who is wed."),
("sub", "6.2 The children of the bridechamber"),
("p", "Jesus uses the same figure when he is asked why his disciples do not fast. “Can the children of the bridechamber mourn, as long as the bridegroom is with them? but the days will come, when the bridegroom shall be taken from them, and then shall they fast” (Matthew 9:15). Mark and Luke record the saying in the same sense (Mark 2:19–20). Those who are with the bridegroom are called the children of the bridechamber, which is to say the wedding company. Jesus does not call them the bride. He calls himself the bridegroom, and he foretells that he will be taken from them. The claim to the title is plain, and the title of the one beside him is the title of a companion."),
("sub", "6.3 The king’s son and the wedding"),
("p", "The parable of the wedding feast opens with the kingdom: “The kingdom of heaven is like unto a certain king, which made a marriage for his son” (Matthew 22:2). The servants are sent “to call them that were bidden to the wedding: and they would not come” (Matthew 22:3). A second sending follows, and the answer is contempt and violence, and the king’s response is judgment on the city of the murderers (Matthew 22:4–7). Then the invitation goes out into the highways, and “the wedding was furnished with guests” (Matthew 22:10). The parable ends with the words, “For many are called, but few are chosen” (Matthew 22:14)."),
("p", "The parable has a king, a son, servants, those who were bidden, other guests and a man without a garment. It has no bride. Its subject is who sits at the table, and the answer is given in terms of the call and the response. If the bride were the company of those who respond, the parable would have no need to say that the guests are guests. Its silence about the bride is the silence of a figure that is already filled by the prophets and does not need to be told again."),
("sub", "6.4 The ten virgins and the returning lord"),
("p", "The parable of the ten virgins is also set in the kingdom of heaven. The virgins “took their lamps, and went forth to meet the bridegroom” (Matthew 25:1). At midnight the cry goes up, “Behold, the bridegroom cometh; go ye out to meet him” (Matthew 25:6), and “they that were ready went in with him to the marriage: and the door was shut” (Matthew 25:10). The lesson is given at once: “Watch therefore, for ye know neither the day nor the hour wherein the Son of man cometh” (Matthew 25:13). The virgins are those who wait and go in with him. They are attendants. The bride is again not among them."),
("p", "Luke records a parable of the same kind from the opposite side. The servants are told to be “like unto men that wait for their lord, when he will return from the wedding; that when he cometh and knocketh, they may open unto him immediately” (Luke 12:36). The lord returns from the wedding, and the blessing is on the servants who are found watching. In every Gospel use of the figure the bridegroom is a person, the wedding is an event that has a time, and those who hear the parable are told what attitude to take toward it. None of them is told that she is the wife."),
("tbl", "The table follows the Gospel uses of the figure.", [
    ("John 3:28", "“I am not the Christ, but that I am sent before him”", "John distinguishes himself from the Christ"),
    ("John 3:29", "“He that hath the bride is the bridegroom: but the friend of the bridegroom”", "The bridegroom has the bride, and the friend is another"),
    ("Matt 9:15", "“Can the children of the bridechamber mourn, as long as the bridegroom is with them?”", "Jesus takes the title of bridegroom for himself"),
    ("Matt 22:2", "“a certain king, which made a marriage for his son”", "The parable names a king, a son and a marriage"),
    ("Matt 22:3", "“to call them that were bidden to the wedding: and they would not come”", "The first guests are those already bidden"),
    ("Matt 22:14", "“For many are called, but few are chosen”", "The call and the choice are the parable’s point"),
    ("Matt 25:6", "“Behold, the bridegroom cometh; go ye out to meet him”", "The virgins go out to meet the bridegroom"),
    ("Luke 12:36", "“men that wait for their lord, when he will return from the wedding”", "The servants wait for the return"),
  ], [0.14, 0.52, 0.34]),
("sub", "6.5 What this chapter does not say"),
("bul", [
    "It does not say that every detail of a parable is an identification. The parables are told to teach readiness and the call, and the volume draws from them only what the figure itself carries.",
    "It does not say that the virgins or the guests of Matthew 22 and 25 are the same company, or that the wedding of Matthew 22 and the marriage of the Lamb are the same event. Chapter 12 returns to the guests.",
    "It does not say that the bride is absent from the Gospels because there is no bride. It says the Gospels leave the bride where the prophets put her.",
  ]),
("call", "Where the argument stands", "John, speaking in the prophets’ terms, names Jesus as the bridegroom and himself as the friend. Jesus takes the title and calls his companions the children of the bridechamber. The parables of the wedding never name a bride among the guests, the servants or the virgins."),
]
